    <div class="callout task">
      <div class="callout-title">Six words you need today</div>
      <p>Not a list to memorise. Each of these has a moment in today's block where you will actually meet it, and that moment is named so you can catch it when it arrives.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>source</strong>: anything you get information from. A paper, a news article, a person, a photograph, an AI.<br><em>Today: all of it. You will judge four different sources before you leave.</em></li>
        <li><strong>primary source</strong>: original evidence, made at the time by the people who were there or who did the measuring.<br><em>Today: the UNU report. It holds the actual numbers.</em></li>
        <li><strong>secondary source</strong>: someone else's summary or interpretation of primary sources.<br><em>Today: the AP article. It reports on the UNU report, so you are reading about the numbers rather than reading the numbers.</em></li>
        <li><strong>OPVL</strong>: the four questions this course asks of every source. Origin, Purpose, Value, Limitations.<br><em>Today: the whole-class work on the AP article, and the exit ticket asks you to name all four.</em></li>
        <li><strong>bias</strong>: a leaning that shapes how a source presents information. Every source has some, so the useful question is which way it leans and how much.<br><em>Today: the AP article quotes industry voices saying the cost is worth it. Careful, "it might be biased" on its own is not a Limitation. Which way, and because of what?</em></li>
        <li><strong>disclosure (AI)</strong>: telling your reader how and where you used AI.<br><em>Today: the handout on your desk. One of those two texts came with a disclosure statement and one did not.</em></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout task">
      <div class="callout-title">What to do with them</div>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Now, in your Field Notebook, two minutes.</strong> Write the six words down the margin. Next to each, one line: what you think it means. Guess. Nobody collects this and being wrong now is the point.</li>
        <li><strong>During the block.</strong> When a word turns up, tick it. If what it turned out to mean was different from your guess, star it instead. The stars are the ones worth keeping.</li>
        <li><strong>Once your Class Notebook exists, later this block.</strong> These six words are your first entry, written as Power Notes under today's entry header.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Power Notes work for vocabulary because the shape already fits: the word is a <strong>Power 1</strong>, the meaning in your own words is a <strong>Power 2</strong> (a Power 2 is a definition), and where you met it is a <strong>Power 3</strong> (a detail underneath). So this entry is you rehearsing the format on six words you already met, before Block 2 asks you to use it on a research paper.</p>
      <div class="power-example">
        <div class="pe-topic">Topic: Vocabulary, U0 B1</div>
        <div class="p1">1. secondary source</div>
        <div class="p2">2. someone else's write-up of evidence they did not gather themselves</div>
        <div class="p3">3. met it: the AP article, reporting numbers the UNU report measured</div>
        <div class="p1">1. bias</div>
        <div class="p2">2. the lean in how a source tells it, not the same as being wrong</div>
        <div class="p3">3. met it: the industry voices in the AP article saying the cost is worth it</div>
      </div>
      <p><strong>One rule for the Power 2 line:</strong> it has to be in your own words. If it matches the handout word for word you have copied, not learned. The Power 3 line is the one that shows you were actually here today.</p>
    </div>

