<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: first OPVL together</h2>
  <p class="sub">Teacher-led whole-class activity. No choice board today. We do this together first, so you can hear what the thinking sounds like before you try it alone.</p>
  <div class="choice-intro">
    <strong>Learning Intention:</strong> {{ learningIntention | safe }}
  </div>
  <figure class="content-figure">
    <img src="/assets/img/u0/u0-opvl-quadrant.svg" alt="OPVL quadrant: Origin asks who made this, when, and what kind of thing it is; Purpose asks why it exists and for whom; Value asks what it is genuinely good for answering; Limitations asks what it cannot tell you.">
  </figure>
  <div class="callout activity">
    <div class="callout-title">Finish the handout (about 20 minutes)</div>
    <p>Straight on from the bell ringer, with the handout still in front of you.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>With a partner: did you mark them the same way? Where you disagree, whose evidence is harder to argue with, and why?</li>
      <li>Between you, list every feature you believe is a reliable sign that a <em>human</em> wrote something. Be specific. "It sounds natural" is not a feature.</li>
      <li>Stop there. We put the tallies and your lists on the board together before anyone turns a page.</li>
      <li>On the cue, Part 2. You get the second sheet then: read what each author said about AI use, answer the three questions, then stop again.</li>
      <li>On the second cue, Part 3.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The question we land on together, and the one Part 3 asks: when you cannot tell by reading, what is left?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="choice">
    <div class="choice-title">The source we OPVL together</div>
    <div class="choice-detail">Seth Borenstein, <a href="https://apnews.com/article/ai-data-centers-environment-climate-footprint-a792f184a9f2833b5388dbae8b41ca95" target="_blank" rel="noopener">'Energy, water use and pollution of AI and data centers rival most countries'</a> (AP News, June 2026). A UN report says the infrastructure behind AI uses electricity and water at the scale of entire countries; industry voices in the same article say it's worth it. We work through OPVL on this together, including on the tools this course itself uses. Printed excerpt (about 400 words) provided in class; full article linked. If you want the primary source the article summarizes, the <a href="/units/unit-0/unu-report-key-points/">selected key points from the UNU-INWEH report</a> are on this site. Comparing how OPVL changes across source types is <a href="/units/unit-0/block-2/">Block 2</a>; today is one source, done properly.</div>
  </div>
  <figure class="content-figure">
    <img src="/assets/img/u0/u0-energy-chart.svg" alt="Bar chart from UNU report data: data centers used 448 terawatt-hours of electricity in 2025 with AI's share at 20 percent, projected to reach 945 terawatt-hours by 2030 with AI's share at 40 percent; a dashed reference line marks the annual residential electricity of all of Sub-Saharan Africa, about 172 terawatt-hours.">
    <figcaption>Drawn only from numbers in the UNU report (pp. 8–10), which means you can check every part of this chart against the source. Try.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="callout" style="margin-top: 16px;">
    <div class="callout-title">Set up your Class Notebook (10 min, today)</div>
